{
  "case_id": "2022-21-806",
  "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:53 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "LAWRENCE S. ROBBINS, ESQUIRE, New York, New York; on behalf of the Petitioners.\nTHOMAS M. FISHER, Solicitor General, Indianapolis, Indiana; for Indiana, et al., as amici curiae, supporting the Petitioners.\nBENJAMIN W. SNYDER, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting neither party.\nANDREW T. TUTT, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "States are therefore entitled, in our view, to clear notice that they will be subject to such private lawsuits if they expect -- if they accept Spending Clause money. Such a clear notice rule comports with the federalism and separation-of-powers principles at stake in these cases, and it accords as well with the common law treatment of third-party beneficiary claims at the time Section 1983 was enacted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: But isn't that a shift from --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Thomas M. Fisher",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0001-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, they frustrate informed state assessment of Spending Clause programs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF THOMAS M. FISHER FOR INDIANA, ET AL., AS AMICI CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE PETITIONERS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You don't read 1983 as being an express right of action?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yeah, but you're -- you're skipping ahead. Now I -- I'm just talking about what Congress would have intended at the time that 1983 was enacted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For half a century, this Court has recognized that Section 1983 means what it says, providing an express cause of action that by default applies to rights created by any federal statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The first is that it is an extensive system of remedies that include enforcement mechanisms by the Secretary himself."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When you rely on the fact that 90 percent of the nursing homes are private, I'm sure you anticipate what's going to come next."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0002-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In that context, Congress said that the Secretary would have responsibility for certifying state facilities and gave the Secretary the ability to impose enforcement remedies at state-operated facilities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew T. Tutt",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The difference between our position and the United States's position in this case is unbelievably narrow. The United States agrees that this Court should not overrule decades of precedent holding that rights secured by Spending Clause laws are enforceable under Section 1983. And the United States agrees that FNHRA creates two enforceable rights. And we are obviously right about those points."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ANDREW T. TUTT ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What do you make of the statement in Gonzaga that the standard for determining whether an individual statutory right exists is \"no less and no more than what is required for Congress to create new rights enforceable under an implied private right of action\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Tutt",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0004-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. TUTT: I -- I don't think so, and I'll give you a few answers. The -- the most important is that 1983 refers to new -- new statutes. You know, Justice Scalia in Blessing talked about the fact that new rights created in new statutes would still be presumptively enforceable under Section 1983."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0004-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 91,
        "start": 91
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Tutt, if you don't use the Spending Clause analogy, what would be the basis or the authority for creating the right in the first instance?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-806",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "73b6c5fef8c5a18d2b80e1079fec70d2014ffb9eb2a1bf2ca39ac1a40148fca2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-73b6c5fef8c5-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: Just really quickly, wouldn't we have to believe that how the right came into being mattered to Congress? I mean, in response to both Justices Thomas and Alito, in order to get to the position of saying Spending Clause legislation is sort of carved out of 1983 because it confers these sort of conditional rights and doesn't confer them directly, doesn't that reduce to saying: We understand that how a right came into being mattered to Congress in order to reach that result?"
    },
    {
